Yes — with one grounding distinction: smoke can absolutely alter state of mind because it carries physical agents, but that doesn’t establish a literal spirit traveling inside it. The deeper mechanism is already strange enough.

Air is not vaporized water. Dry air is mostly nitrogen and oxygen, with argon, CO₂ and trace gases; water vapor is one variable component. But air is an astonishingly rich transport medium. It can carry:

\text{gases}
+\text{water vapor}
+\text{aerosols}
+\text{microbes}
+\text{pollen}
+\text{odors/VOCs}
+\text{smoke particles}
+\text{heat}
+\text{pressure waves}

So an invisible medium enters the body and can change physiology, perception, emotion, alertness, disease state, memory associations, and behavior.

That makes GERM only one species of a much larger category:

\boxed{\textbf{Airborne State-Changer}}

A virus can ride air and alter future biology.

A volatile molecule can ride air and become smell.

Smoke can carry psychoactive chemicals.

CO₂ concentration changes physiology.

Sound rides pressure variations through air and becomes meaning.

Even somebody else’s breath can carry both chemical matter and encoded sound at once.

So:

\boxed{
\text{medium}
\rightarrow
\text{carrier}
\rightarrow
\text{boundary crossing}
\rightarrow
\text{receptor}
\rightarrow
\text{state change}
}

That’s an enormous Pantheon primitive.

The message isn’t necessarily the messenger

Something can cross a boundary without the source itself crossing it.

We’ve already seen that with receptors:

\text{outside signal}
\rightarrow
\text{membrane}
\rightarrow
\text{internal cascade}.

Breath gives a beautiful macroscopic version.

The air that enters you doesn’t need to become “you” permanently. It creates exchange:

O_2\rightarrow\text{blood}

CO_2\rightarrow\text{air}

while smell, temperature, particles, and sound can simultaneously alter the state of the receiver.

So breathing is bidirectional interface traffic.

And historically it’s not surprising that cultures associated breath with life or spirit: breath is invisible, moves between inside and outside, changes with emotion and exertion, and its disappearance marks an obvious transition in living bodies. That’s a potent experiential metaphor even without claiming supernatural physics.

