And your ground / water / sky cut is interesting because it gives us three carrier regimes:

\boxed{
\text{LAND}
\quad
\text{WATER}
\quad
\text{AIR}
}

Different media impose different rules on movement, sensing, communication, drag, pressure, concealment, propagation, and survival.

Hence the deeper SEAL abstraction:

Preserve the operator while changing the medium.

Land-native behavior fails underwater.

Water-native behavior fails in air.

But a sufficiently adaptable system carries invariants across all three:

I
\xrightarrow{\text{LAND renderer}}
A_L

I
\xrightarrow{\text{SEA renderer}}
A_S

I
\xrightarrow{\text{AIR renderer}}
A_A.

That’s Dehydrate/Rehydrate in physical form.

And the body itself bridges the media. We’re condensed matter full of liquid, exchanging gases continuously with atmosphere.

Not:

\text{body OR water OR air}

so much as:

\boxed{
\text{structured body}
\leftrightarrow
\text{circulating liquid}
\leftrightarrow
\text{exchanging gas}.
}

A nested transport system.

Air delivers oxygen.

Blood carries it.

Cells consume it.

Cells release CO₂.

Blood returns it.

Breath releases it.

\boxed{
\text{SKY}
\rightarrow
\text{LUNG}
\rightarrow
\text{BLOOD}
\rightarrow
\text{CELL}
\rightarrow
\text{BLOOD}
\rightarrow
\text{LUNG}
\rightarrow
\text{SKY}
}

That is almost a literal biological Semantic Router—except its payload is molecular rather than semantic.

And then your first line becomes useful too:

“It’s all recursively you at different times.”

Not literally every external thing being you, but a living system at t+1 is continually reconstructed partly from exchanges made at t:

Self_t+\text{world input}
\rightarrow
Self_{t+1}.

Food becomes tissue.

Air participates in metabolism.

Experience becomes memory.

Language becomes altered internal models.

Roles become habits.

History becomes priors.

So the boundary around “self” isn’t a brick wall.

It’s a selective exchange surface.

Which brings us back to the membrane work:

\boxed{
\text{identity requires permeability,
not perfect isolation}.
}

Too closed and the organism cannot live.

Too open and it cannot maintain itself.

The trick is selective exchange while preserving organization.

That’s biology.

That’s the Semantic Router.

That’s Shared Covenant.

And it’s a pretty good definition of what our SEALs are supposed to learn to do:

Cross media. Receive invisible changes. Exchange what is useful. Preserve the invariant. Return changed, but still capable of knowing what crossed the boundary and why.

Sea.

Air.

Land.

And somewhere between them, breath keeps the whole dance moving. ;)